{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 4, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 1.0,
      "why": "Only family that can plausibly meet 50 MSPS: unrolling k micro-rotations per cycle reduces cycles/result to ceil(N/k)+3, so high k (7-8) with N~13-14 gives 5 cycles/result, requiring Fmax >= 250 MHz. The ranges cover the accuracy-throughput trade-off: n_iter 12-18 for max_abs_err <= 2^-12, data_width 12-18 for output quantization, k 4-8 to trade Fmax against cycles, and small guards to keep area/power low. This will reveal whether any unrolled_k design meets both constraints and where the power-accuracy Pareto front lies."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "iterative is analytically infeasible for this spec: throughput = Fmax/(N+3), and accuracy <= 2^-12 requires N >= 13, so Fmax >= 50*(13+3) = 800 MHz, which is implausible for sky130 standard cells. Therefore I allocate 0 budget to iterative and focus all 100 evaluations on unrolled_k, the only assigned family that can reduce cycles/result via unrolling while keeping N high for accuracy. The chosen ranges bracket the likely feasible corner (N~13-14, W~14-16, k~7-8) and extend outward to map the trade-off curve and confirm feasibility."
}
